[Student "S"]
{[show NS]

South is to play 6NT. West leads the \S3.

Make a Plan, then click NEXT. [NEXT]



Winners:   \S = 4    \H = 1  \D = 5    \C = 1 Total = 11

You need one more winner from the \H or \C suit. The straight finesse of the \HK offers a 50% chance of success. The double finesse of the \C K Q offers a 75% chance of success. It's a no-brainer. Run 4 \Ss and 5 \Ds ending in your hand. Be very careful not to discard a \C from your hand.

Click NEXT. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:S3,N:SJ,E:S4,S:S5,N:SQ,E:S6,S:S9,W:S7,N:SA,E:H4,S:H2,W:S8,N:S2,E:H5,S:SK,W:ST,S:D7,W:D2,N:DA,E:D3,N:DK,E:D4,S:D8,W:D6,N:D5,E:D9,S:DQ,W:H3,S:DJ,W:C4,N:H7,E:HT,S:DT,W:C9,N:H6,E:C2]

You have run the \Ss and \Ds and are in your hand. Which card do you lead? [choose-card any:C8,C3]

[showcards W:C6]

West plays low. Which card do you play from dummy? [choose-card CT]

[showcards E:CK]



A small \C, playing the \C10 when West plays low: the first of your two \C finesses. Let's say East wins the \CK and returns a \H.

Click NEXT. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:C6,E:CK] [showcards E:HJ]

East returns the \HJ. Which card do you play? [choose-card HA]

[showcards W:H8 N:H9]



<b>DO NOT FINESSE THE \H.</b>Stick with your original 75% plan. Take the \HA and play your small \C, finessing the \CJ. This is why you couldn't discard a \C earlier.

Click NEXT to see the complete Deal. [NEXT]

[RESET]

[show NESW]

[clear-commentary]

The single \H finesse is an inferior play because its success depends on East holding a specific card, the \HK.

The double \C finesse is much better because its success depends on West holding either of two cards, the \CQ <b>OR</b> the \CK.}
[Play "W"]
