{[show NS]

[showcards W:DK] South is to play 4\S. West leads the \DK.

Make a Plan, then choose dummy's card to this trick. [choose-card any:D6,D5,D3]



[showcards E:D2 S:D4] East plays the \D2 and you the \D4.

Losers:   \S = 1    \H = 1  \D = 1    \C = 1 Total = 4

West has made another great lead. Any other suit would have given you a free finesse. You should duck the first \D. It is very possible that West has led from \D K Q 10 and will be afraid to continue for fear you have the \DJ in your hand. You'd be very happy to see him switch to any other suit. But he doesn't. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:DK,E:D2,S:D4] [showcards W:DQ]

West continues with the \DQ. Which card do you play from dummy? [choose-card DA]

[showcards E:D9 S:D8]

You take it with dummy's \DA. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:DQ,E:D9,S:D8]



You are in dummy for the first, last, and only time. You need to try a finesse right now, but which one? Which card do you lead from dummy? [choose-card any:CJ,CT,C9,C5]

[showcards E:C3]

East plays low. Which card do you play? [choose-card CQ]

[showcards W:C2] At first you might think it makes no difference but, in fact, there is only one reasonable choice. If you finesse the \SK successfully you may still have a \S loser. If you finesse the \HK successfully you will surely still have a \H loser. But if you finesse the \CK successfully you will eliminate your \C loser.

Clickfor the complete Deal. [NEXT]

[RESET]

[show NESW]

[clear-commentary]

What a great layout! Three possible finesses and every one of them would work.

But as you can see, only the \C finesse will actually save you a trick.}
[Play "W"]
DK

