[Student "S"]
{[show NS]

[showcards W:HK N:H6 E:H3] South is to play 6\S. West leads the \HK, dummy plays the \H6 and East the \H3.

Make a Plan, then play a card from your hand. [choose-card HA]



Losers:   \S = 0    \H = 1  \D = 2    \C = 0 Total = 3

Your only hope for ridding yourself of two of those losers is dummy's \C suit. One possibility would be to finesse West for the \CK, and if that works, discard your \H loser on the \CA. You could then establish another \C winner for one of the \D losers. The problem with that plan is that if the finesse fails the defense will immediately take the setting \H trick.

A better plan is a ruffing finesse. Win the first trick with your \HA and pull trumps with your \S K Q. 

Click NEXT. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:HK,N:H6,E:H3,S:SK,W:S2,N:S3,E:H7,S:SQ,W:ST,N:S7,E:D4]



Which card do you lead now? [choose-card C7]

[showcards W:C3]

West plays low. Which card do you play from dummy? [choose-card CA]

[showcards E:C2]

Your \C7 to dummy's \CA. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:C3,E:C2]

Which card do you lead from dummy? [choose-card CQ]

The \CQ from dummy: a ruffing finesse. If East plays the \CK you will ruff, enter dummy, and discard two red losers on the \C J 10. If East plays low instead, you discard your \H loser. West may be able to win the trick but you can ruff a \H return, and dummy will have two good \Cs on which you can discard 2 \D losers.

Clickfor the complete Deal. [NEXT]

[RESET]

[show NESW]

[clear-commentary]

Ruffing finesses sometimes provide you the best opportunity, like this time.

But they are easy to overlook, mainly because we don't use them often.}
[Play "W"]
HK

