{[show NS]

[showcards W:H3 N:H4 E:HJ] South is to play 3NT. West leads the \H3, dummy plays the \H4 and East the \HJ.

Make a Plan, then play a card from your hand. [choose-card any:HK,HA]



Winners:   \S = 0    \H = 2  \D = 2    \C = 4 Total = 8

First things first. Win the first \H, do not hold up. West's \H3 indicates he has led a 4-card suit so a hold-up wouldn't help you. And if you did hold up they might pocket their \H trick and switch to \Ds, a suit you are no better off in.

You need to get one more winner and it will have to be a \S winner. 



[PLAY W:H3,N:H4,E:HJ]

Which card do you lead? [choose-card any:S8,S7,S3]

[showcards W:S4]

West plays low. Which card do you play from dummy? [choose-card ST]

[showcards E:SK]

You could lead toward the \SQ, winning a trick if West holds BOTH the \SA and \SK. Or you could finesse the \S10, gaining a trick if West holds the \SJ. The second is more likely. Suppose East wins the \SK. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:S4,E:SK] [showcards E:H9]

East returns a \H. Which card do you play? [choose-card any:HA,HK]

[PLAY E:H9,W:H7,N:H6]

West follows and dummy plays the \H6. Which card do you lead now? [choose-card any:S8,S7,S3]

[showcards W:SJ]

West plays the \SJ. Which card do you play from dummy? [choose-card SQ]

[showcards E:S6]

The \SQ wins the trick. If the \SQ wins, take your 9 tricks and run.

Clickfor the complete Deal. [NEXT]

[RESET]

[show NESW]

[clear-commentary]

Although this doesn't look like a normal A Q finesse it really is about the same.

Whenever the \SA and \SK are in different hands, the only thing that matters is the location of the \SJ. 

So it is basically a 50% chance.}
[Play "W"]
