{[show NS]

South is to play 4\S. West leads the \D2. The defenders win 3 \D tricks, then play a \C.

Make a Plan, then click NEXT. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:D2,N:D4,E:DA,S:D3,E:DT,S:DQ,W:DK,N:D6,W:DJ,N:D8,E:D7,S:D5,W:C2,N:C3,E:C7,S:CJ]



Losers:   \S = 0 or 1    \H = 0  \D = 3    \C = 0 Total = 3 or 4

Since you have already lost 3 \D tricks you cannot also lose a \S trick. That means East will have to have the \SK and it also means that you will have to be careful with your finessing.

Click NEXT. [NEXT]



You are in your hand, having won the \C switch. Which card do you lead? [choose-card H4]

[showcards W:H3]

West plays low. Which card do you play from dummy? [choose-card HA]

[showcards E:H2]

Over to dummy's \HA. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:H3,E:H2]

Which card do you lead from dummy? [choose-card SQ]

[showcards E:S3]

East plays low. Which card do you play? [choose-card any:SJ,ST]

[showcards W:S7]

The \SQ from dummy, and the \SJ (or 10) from your hand when East plays low.

Click NEXT. [NEXT]

[PLAY E:S3,W:S7]



The \SQ held the trick so you are still in dummy. Which card do you lead from dummy? [choose-card S9]

[showcards E:S4]

East doesn't cover. Which card do you play? [choose-card S8]

[showcards W:C4]

The \S9, playing your \S8 under it. West shows out on this trick and you are glad you played so carefully. Play one more \S, finessing East's \SK again, then play your \SA and the rest of your winners.

Clickfor the complete Deal. [NEXT]

[RESET]

[show NESW]

[clear-commentary]

If you had played the \SQ and carelessly followed with the \S8 on your first finesse you would have gone down. You would have had to win the second finesse in your hand and would have no dummy entry to let you take the third finesse.

True, the chances of East holding 4 \Ss to the King are pretty small, but it costs you nothing to protect yourself against that holding. 

And the rewards are great.}
[Play "W"]
D2

