{[show NS]

[showcards W:HQ N:H2 E:H6] South is to play 3NT. West leads the \HQ, dummy plays the \H2 and East the \H6.

Make a Plan, then play a card from your hand. [choose-card any:HA,HK]



Winners:   \S = 1    \H = 2  \D = 1    \C = 3 Total = 7

You need two more winners and \Ds is your longest combined suit. But you can only establish one extra winner in \Ds, and before you could even do that the defenders would have taken a bunch of \H tricks.

So you must turn to the \Ss for the extra two winners.

[PLAY W:HQ,N:H2,E:H6]



You won the first \H. Which card do you lead? [choose-card any:S7,S4]

[showcards W:S2]

West plays low. Which card do you play from dummy? [choose-card SA]

[showcards E:S9]

Which \S was right? If you play the \SQ you will go down, even if West has the \SK. He would cover and you would be held to just 2 \S tricks.

The only way to get 3 tricks from this holding is to play East for the \SK. So play a \S to dummy's \SA and then a small \S back toward your \S Q J. [NEXT]

[PLAY W:S2,E:S9]

Which card do you lead from dummy? [choose-card any:S6,S3]

[showcards E:SK]

East puts on the \SK. Which card do you play? [choose-card any:S7,S4]

[showcards W:S5]

You are home-free.

Clickfor the full Deal. [NEXT]

[RESET]

[show NESW]

[clear-commentary]

You were a little lucky that East's \SK fell on the second round. 

If it had not dropped you would just have played a third round of \Ss, hoping for a 3-3 split in the suit.

Clickagain. [NEXT]

[PLAY E:C2,W:S2]

[clear-commentary]

You could also have gotten 3 \S winners with this distribution.}
[Play "W"]
